Bus Hobbs drove the van, being the youngest at sixty-six, with the boy up beside him on two cushions. Behind them the machines came two abreast for three miles. They crossed the Shawneetown bridge that way and went through Louisville, and people got out of their cars on the overpasses of Interstate 71 to watch them go under. Mrs. Lashley was on the ambulance dock in her sweater when they came up the hill. She looked down the line as far as it went. Pike. You brought company. Yes, ma'am. He told her how it had been. Take them around to Mrs. Hauck. Mrs. Hauck came up from the basement to the front steps with the tape around her neck. She looked at five hundred men standing in the street in yellow denim with their hair cut ragged. Pike, you have people now? He told it again. Is that the way it was? she asked them. Yes, ma'am, they said. Then hear me. One day, the way you are standing there this evening, is worth more than a hundred years of what you were at. She folded her hands on her stomach, as she must have stood to say it in the sixth grade, and gave them a thing she had had by heart ever since. It is not growing like a tree
